{
  "name": "Brain-eating amoebas kill by turning your body against you",
  "parent_id": 203313787796667,
  "group_id": "f5ffc3ba-2a46-4bc9-b497-3fbde763d867",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-30T14:49:50.837Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-07-30T14:49:50.915Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-30T14:49:50.902Z",
  "id": 203707968908941,
  "uuid": "4482a07f-b2eb-447e-af51-572fea867102",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "0cd5684e-9e12-49d7-8d5f-3e10d5bac59c",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "dd993829-5815-c5bf-f3fd-4f42056a0c7e",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "But for someone swimming in these pools, it can be a death sentence-if they get water up their nose, the amoeba heads for the brain and starts to destroy brain cells and tissue, which is ultimately fatal.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Is there something else at work? ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Abdul Mannan Baig, Senior Instructor, Department of Biological and Biomedical Sciences thinks so.  He was interviewed by the New Scientist, a UK-based weekly international magazine, for his research on Naegleria fowleri",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Pathogenesis of amoebic encephalitis: Are the amoebas being credited to an 'inside job' done by the host immune response?' published in Acta Tropica.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "In Pakistan, we have something like 20 deaths per year (due to Naegleria fowleri)\", said Dr Baig. \"The brain reacts swiftly and intensely to the parasitic infection and it is the body's own immune response which contributes to the parasite's lethality.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "He suggests that infected patients should first be treated with immunosuppressive drugs, and then with drugs that target the deadly parasites. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
